  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 251 PM CDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nMostly cloudy skies are occurring this afternoon across most of the \narea with isolated light rain showers occurring ahead of an \napproaching warm front and associated low pressure. Dewpoint temps \nand PWAT will be increasing this afternoon and evening as the warm \nfront continues to lift north, with dewpoints in the mid 60s to low \n70s by this evening. As a lobe of vorticity at 500mb associated with \nthe vertically-stacked low pressure over the Canadian Prairies \npropagates through the CWA tonight into Monday morning, this will \nlikely (50-75% chance) bring showers and thunderstorms to the area. \n\nPrevious forecasts indicated a potential for isolated severe \nthunderstorms in north-central MN as this warm front lifted north. \nHowever, recent hi-res models runs have reduced the severe potential \nto the point where severe thunderstorms are not anticipated in the \nDuluth CWA this evening through tonight. While MUCAPE values will \nlikely be increasing to an impressive 2,000 J/kg overnight into \nearly Monday morning, this instability is unlikely to be realized. A \nsubstantial low-level cap will increase sfc-based CIN up to 400 J/kg \nand dry air in the low to mid levels will likely inhibit severe \nconvection. With this being said, a strong thunderstorm capable of \nproducing up to nickel sized hail will still be possible with gusty \nwinds of 50 mph or less being a secondary concern given the capping.\n\nOn Monday, an occluded front is anticipated to sweep through the \nCWA. This will bring a wind shift with the breezy easterly winds \ntoday, which have been causing dangerous swimming conditions around \nthe Twin Ports, switching to the west. Despite MUCAPE values \nremaining elevated through Monday morning into the early afternoon, \nsignificant capping and dry air entrainment in the low to mid levels \nis currently anticipated to keep things dry in the afternoon. CAMs \nare all in solid agreement for minimal to no precipitation \ndevelopment along the occluded front on Monday, so kept the forecast \ndry. \n\nAs the vertically-stacked low pressure propagates east-northeast \ntowards Hudson Bay on Monday into Wednesday, this will place the CWA \nin a fairly benign pattern with a mid-level ridge building. The bulk \nof precipitation associated with a shortwave lifting north from the \nHigh Plains on Tuesday night into Wednesday will likely remain just \nsouth of the CWA. Southern portions of the WI CWA has an up to 30% \nchance for scattered showers and thunderstorms, with QPF progged to \nbe on the light side at a few hundredths of an inch. Elsewhere, dry \nconditions are forecast on Monday through Wednesday with \nunseasonably warm temps. \n\nAdditional chances for showers and thunderstorms arrive Thursday and \ncontinue on and off into the weekend as a series of shortwaves \npotentially propagate through the area. Signals for decent \nprecipitation are not particularly strong, with most global ensemble \nmembers for 24 hour QPF showing relatively light amounts under 0.5\" \nlate in the week. Temperatures remain on the warm side for the \nlatter half of the week into next weekend, with highs generally in \nthe upper 70s to mid 80s. Certainly a warm start to September!",
